+# Candidate admission for cycle creation, bumps and sync-back
+
+Read this before creating a release branch, publishing a bump or landing a sync-back.
+Read `../../_shared/base-green.md` and `../../_shared/validation-gate.md` for the
+canonical evidence and resource rules. This file specifies the release transitions;
+it does not grant merge, publication, administrative or protected-instruction approval.
+Reuse authorization already given for the exact action; ask only for missing authority.
+
+## Bind the evidence before using it
+
+Record repository, target branch, live target SHA `B`, candidate/head SHA `H`, tree,
+PR number and draft state, GitHub test-merge SHA/tree when present, workflow source
+SHA/path, event, run ID/attempt, check name/App ID, terminal result and observation time.
+For a branch cut, record the certified source SHA/tree `S` separately. For sync-back,
+also record the exact `main` SHA `M`. A PR API `.base.sha` snapshot is not a live branch
+tip: query the branch/ref itself and re-read it immediately before publication.
+
+Use the candidate's `config/quality/admission-policy.json` and current workflows to
+enumerate required lanes. In the reviewed OmniRoute policy the aggregate names are
+`Gate / CI` and `Gate / Quality`, issued by GitHub Actions (App ID `15368`); verify that
+binding and the workflow/policy source at execution time. A matching name or App ID
+alone does not establish the policy that ran. Also read all effective rulesets and
+classic branch protections for the actual target, including creation, PR, strictness,
+review, signing and merge-method constraints. Missing native checks are a control gap,
+not permission to skip the policy or silently edit the rules.
+
+Before editing, verify every referenced script and flag against source on the selected
+candidate. Skills may be installed ahead of a branch's tooling. A missing required helper
+means HOLD for compatibility repair; do not import a different checkout's implementation
+or invent an equivalent flag to continue the release.
+
+Require terminal `success` from both aggregate verdicts and every applicable required
+lane. Preserve the policy's explicit exclusions, but do not accept a draft fast path,
+unexpected skip/neutral, queued/cancelled/timed-out/missing result or `action_required`
+as PASS. The latter is pending execution authorization, not contributor failure.
+An issue's status, a clean merge, five displayed checks, a local quick result and a
+full observer profile are not interchangeable with complete candidate acceptance.
+Use `../../validate-release-green/SKILL.md` for exact local profile and comparison
+binding; record required lanes outside that profile separately. A changed source,
+target, candidate, workflow or policy invalidates the corresponding acceptance.
+
+For PR admission, verify the commit GitHub evaluates: it may be the test-merge commit
+rather than the head. Bind both and prove the checked tree corresponds to the proposed
+landing; do not relabel a merge-tree PASS as validation of a different source tree.
+Only eligible event evidence counts. A `workflow_dispatch` job on a PR head does not
+satisfy a ruleset's required workflow check; a merge queue needs `merge_group`.
+[GitHub required-check evaluation](https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/how-tos/merge-and-close-pull-requests/troubleshooting-required-status-checks).
+
+## Release cut and bump: four separate transitions
+
+1. **SOURCE_READY** — freeze and pin `S`; prove the exact source tree against the
+   applicable policy. If the frozen source is red or its evidence is missing, keep the
+   cut and retargeting on HOLD while repairing the base in its own authorized task.
+   Do not silently substitute `main`. A PR test-merge with a different tree is not `S`.
+2. **BRANCH_CREATED** — once authorized and eligible under the current creation rules,
+   create the new release ref at exactly `S`, without adding a bump commit. Re-read the
+   remote ref and require equality to `S`. A GitHub rejection is HOLD: preserve the
+   response classification and do not bypass, change enforcement or try another base.
+   Existing branch names are resume inputs, not proof these transitions happened.
+3. **BUMP_ADMITTED** — prepare `/version-bump` in an owned worktree on a dedicated task
+   branch based on that release tip. Publish only the task branch and open a **non-draft**
+   PR against the new release. Complete exact-candidate admission and use the authorized
+   normal merge path. Never push the new bump directly to the integration branch.
+4. **NEXT_READY** — read back the bump's actual landed SHA/tree and the applicable
+   complete acceptance evidence. If the merge method produced a different SHA/tree,
+   establish that identity's evidence instead of carrying the PR label forward.
+   Only then open/reuse the living draft release PR to `main`, change the default branch,
+   retarget the selected queue and announce the new cycle ready. Re-read each mutation;
+   drift or a partial handoff remains HOLD with completed substeps recorded.
+
+The living draft PR is release-note organization, not admission evidence. Do not depend
+on release-branch push/create events to produce missing checks: inspect the triggers;
+the reviewed CI/Quality workflows run on PRs and only `main` for push. Creation under
+`do_not_enforce_on_create: false` and the exact landing path require an authorized GitHub
+trial before claiming operational compatibility. This document is not that trial.
+
+## Sync-back: keep the tested merge commit
+
+Prepare a dedicated task branch from `B` in an owned worktree, merge pinned `M`, resolve
+conflicts with source evidence and run the required validation. Keep `B` and `M` ancestral
+to the final `H`. Publish the task branch and open a non-draft PR with base equal to the
+live next-cycle branch and head exactly `H`. Re-run admission when that tuple changes.
+Strict policy requires the candidate to include the current target; do not weaken it.
+
+After exact-candidate admission, fresh rules/ref readback and existing landing authority,
+land by **fast-forward, never squash**. Re-read the target and `main` immediately before
+the push; if they differ from `B`/`M`, reconcile and revalidate first. A normal non-forced
+push preserves Git's ancestry check but is not an atomic snapshot of all GitHub state.
+Record the server outcome, fetch and verify the resulting ref equals `H`, and prove both
+captured `M` and freshly fetched `origin/main` are ancestors. If main advances concurrently,
+report the residual sync explicitly. Read the PR's actual merged state instead of assuming
+the push marked it merged. Rejection or ambiguous readback is HOLD; preserve the candidate,
+with no bypass, force push, squash fallback, automatic rule edit or retry loop.
+
+GitHub's PR rule requires an open associated PR, not necessarily an approving review.
+Its warning about manually pushed merge commits is conditional on stale-review/last-push
+settings and matching merge contents. These facts neither forbid every associated FF nor
+guarantee this candidate will be accepted: validate current rules and the actual outcome.
+[GitHub ruleset semantics](https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/available-rules-for-rulesets).
+
+## Ownership and completion
+
+Create/reuse worktrees only after resource and compatibility-lock admission. Verify the
+expected common Git directory, physical root, dedicated branch and source identity before
+the first write; dependencies are physical/hard-linked and hooks must execute. A lifecycle
+fix in a helper does not prove that its caller has resource admission or PR publication.
+Do not invoke a combined merge-and-push script without the separation this contract needs.
+If there is no implemented prepare-only mode, use the reviewed manual candidate procedure;
+do not invent a flag. Quick checks and `--skip-green-gate` cannot waive required acceptance.
+
+Before removing an owned worktree, require no active readers/processes, a clean owned tree,
+and HEAD equal to the published candidate recorded for that task. Preserve unlanded work
+and evidence. Keep instructions touching protected surfaces behind their specific operator
+approval before installation/merge; preparation and read-only validation can continue.
+
+Report each transition with exact identities and evidence, remaining HOLD/UNKNOWN items,
+and the live default/target/PR readback. Branch existence alone never means cycle ready.
